in good shape your health magazine on DW  featuring an interview with a different  expert every week  stress in the city it's a big topic and  with me in the studio today is dr. Mazda  oddly he lives in a big city  he lives in Berlin and he's also  carrying out research on this topic  welcome to in good shape hi  so are you doing research on stress in  the city why is that important well the  global population living in cities is  growing dramatically today every 50% of  mankind is living in urban environments  by 2050 about two-thirds of mankind will  be living in cities so urbanization is  the global change that is occurring on  this planet with a lot of health impact  and we're trying to understand what  urbanization does to our psychological  well-being so it's a major health risk  and what is it that makes life in the  cities so stressful well we do see  stress-related diseases in cities such  as depression which occurs more  frequently in in cities such as  schizophrenia twice as frequent in  cities then in rural environments if it  even comes with a dose response  relationship the greater the city you're  going up in the the larger your risk for  suffering from schizophrenia later as an  adult and this probably has a  stress-related mechanism and the root of  all that is social stress ok social  stress meaning there are too many people  in such a crowded place well social  stress is the combination of social  density on the one hand and social  isolation on the other hand when two of  these two stressors combine the mixture  can become toxic so why is that I mean  I'm am i lonely when I live in a big  city  not necessarily most of us actually most  people rather benefit from living in  large cities they benefit from what is  called the urban advantage but they're  but they're at risk population  like older people like migrants for  example who rather who don't have the  the coping instruments to deal with  isolation or with deal with with density  who can't escape the density of cities  and then this becomes health relevant so  say if you take a look at the brain of  some people living in a big city and at  the brain and people living in rural  areas do you actually see difference in  the brain structure or how it works well  there was a very nice study here from  Germany from Mannheim which showed that  the activity in some regions of the  brain where emotions are processed is  different in city dwellers than in  people from rural areas they show city  dwellers show a large activity stronger  activity in the amygdala which is a very  important brain region which controls  our emotions and what if I would take  some inhabitant of a city and say go get  a farm back buy some piece of land and  get out into a rural area with the brain  change after the the changing in  location well it's probably the first 15  years of our lives that count the the  every single year that you spent growing  growing growing up in a inner city  during those first 15 years increases  your risk for schizophrenia later on so  this is the probably the vulnerable  period you said that people living in  cities shouldn't sit at home behind the  closed door they should go out and live  the city what you mean by that well it's  it's always helpful to to spend as much  time in front of your door than behind  your own doorstep to increase the your  social connections with your peers with  your neighbors and that protects from  social isolation and social isolation is  a big major health risk so if you just  would well social isolation is one of  the important social stressors which is  as harmful for our  as like a smoking or even obesity and as  is social density and spending much time  in front of your doors than behind it  protects but we're not thing of myself  going through building it I can see the  tube going there planes  rushing over my head and it's also  stressful a lot of lots of people and  I'm feeling stressed after this so so  but you say I have to go and and and to  feel it why does this help well it's  it's because it helps to not avoid what  stresses you out but to face it and to  to expose yourself and to concentrate on  different details for example a a walk  through your city or through your  neighborhood even where you focus on the  details on the trees on the on the  streets on the benches that you see it's  helpful to to make the environment more  familiar to you like the awareness you  have when you go through a forest you go  just go through the city and have some  meditation there like this so you found  it a group an interdisciplinary group  with architects neuroscientists all  working together to find some solution  to the problem of the stressful city and  what things are you looking at yeah so  at the beginning of the of this year  we've found it as the forum new urbanism  which is a group of newer of architects  of city planners and neuroscientists  together with the Alfred hair house in  society and we try to put our  methodologies together to to learn from  each other and to in order to better  understand how a perfect city which is  good for our health should be shaped and  as a result you're designing something  like a stress map for the city what's  that about well stress map is something  we're targeting at - because we think  it's it in order to understand in order  to understand what is stressful about  the city you in what city stress is  exactly composed from it can be helpful  to to map it to ask for example a lot of  people inhabitants of the city to to  rate their well-being or their anxiety  or their stress level on an app and as a  result we have something like an  emotional map which helps us to  understand what is where are the  stressful points and sources of city  living dr. Ali thanks so much for being  with us good shape today things
 

